### V4. SQLite atomic-commit semantics: what "atomic save" actually buys you, and its preconditions

- Source: `https://www.sqlite.org/atomiccommit.html` ("Atomic Commit In SQLite"), status 200, last-modified 2026-09-11, ETag `"m6aa3e181s13090"`, full body sha256 `5a5be7b660217c9408c8a81eaf2faa2d08832ed3790f895a0a713916ad856ea1` (77,968 bytes); delivered head 32,768 bytes, sha256 `204cdfe22e90a7933f18a2b74558c90ff12997dd22f16facd6bed4f1885bfc10`.
- Locators: §1 Introduction — "Atomic commit means that either all database changes within a single transaction occur or none of them occur … transactions appear to be atomic even if the transaction is interrupted by an operating system crash or power failure." §2 Hardware Assumptions — "SQLite does not assume that sector writes are atomic"; "We are told that the flush and fsync primitives are broken on some versions of Windows and Linux." §3.11 — "After the database changes are all safely on the mass storage device, the rollback journal file is deleted. This is the instant where the transaction commits … SQLite gives the appearance of having made no changes to the database file or having made the complete set of changes … depending on whether or not the rollback journal file exists"; "Deleting a file is not really an atomic operation, but it appears to be from the point of view of a user process." §9 "Things That Can Go Wrong" enumerates broken locking, incomplete disk flushes, partial file deletions, garbage writes, and "Deleting Or Renaming A Hot Journal" as corruption sources.
- Applicability: this is the authoritative support for a journal-then-swap save design (write sidecar/journal, fsync, then flip). It also carries two cautions a proposal must state honestly: atomicity depends on working fsync on the target OS/filesystem, and recovery logic must tolerate the journal/half-written state (§4 "hot rollback journals", §9.5). Note honestly: this verifies SQLite's own mechanism, not POSIX `rename()` semantics; a "temp file + rename is atomic on POSIX, needs `os.replace` on Windows" claim remains an engineering inference (section 5, W4), and SQLite's own fallback exists as the bounded alternative if the team does not want to reason about filesystem rename semantics.

